UTF-8 for every narrow string, whatever the locale of the process.

One of this project's patches to libarchive, which every build (macOS, Linux and
Windows) applies with patch_libarchive (native/build-config.sh), to the libarchive
release pinned there, in the order that LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES there lists them. A build
fails if any hunk does not apply exactly (no fuzz), and scripts/check-native-updates.sh
will not propose a libarchive release that it does not apply to. See "The libarchive
patches" in native/BUILD.md.

LibArchive.Net passes UTF-8 to libarchive's narrow (char *) functions and decodes
every narrow string it gets back as UTF-8. libarchive instead takes narrow strings to
be in the "current" character set, which it gets from the process locale, and
converts names between that and the character set of an archive.

On macOS and Linux it asks nl_langinfo(CODESET), and converts between narrow and wide
strings with mbrtowc() and wcrtomb(). A .NET process never calls setlocale(), so that
locale is "C", whose character set is ASCII ("US-ASCII" on macOS, "ANSI_X3.4-1968" in
glibc and "ASCII" in musl). Non-ASCII names then fail to convert: the pax, 7-Zip,
ISO 9660 (Joliet) and xar writers warn and store them wrongly or not at all, the 7-Zip,
xar, zip (UTF-8 flag) and Joliet readers return no name, the pax reader warns, the zip
writer leaves out the UTF-8 flag, and wide strings are garbled.

On Windows it takes a code page from the C runtime's locale, and in the "C" locale
it makes a narrow string one byte per character, which holds U+0000-U+00FF only. A
Rock Ridge name in ISO 9660, which is taken from the narrow form, is then Latin-1 if
every character fits, and the ISO 9660 and xar writers misname a non-ASCII parent
directory that they add themselves. In any other locale, names in formats that
declare no character set are converted between the OEM code page and the locale's.

This patch makes the current character set UTF-8 on every platform, without touching
any locale. An hdrcharset option still sets an archive's character set, the other
side of each conversion:

1. default_iconv_charset() returns "UTF-8" as the current character set, as it does
   on Android, instead of asking the locale.
2. archive_wstring_append_from_mbs() and archive_string_append_from_wcs() convert
   between UTF-8 and Unicode code points (wchar_t is 32 bits on Linux and macOS)
   with libarchive's own UTF-8 code instead of mbrtowc() and wcrtomb(). Windows
   builds compile other versions of them, which use the current code page (5).
3. On macOS, names read from an archive are no longer made NFD (decomposed): they
   are NFC, as libarchive makes them on every other platform, so a name written
   composed reads back composed.
4. The zip writer sets the UTF-8 flag (general purpose bit 11) on a non-ASCII name
   when no hdrcharset option says otherwise, as it already did in a UTF-8 locale.
5. On Windows, get_current_codepage() and get_current_oemcp() return CP_UTF8, so
   narrow strings convert to and from UTF-16 as UTF-8 (with MultiByteToWideChar()
   and WideCharToMultiByte()), and the default conversions from and to the OEM code
   page do nothing, as they do on other platforms. As MultiByteToWideChar() rejects
   any flag but MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS for UTF-8,
   archive_wstring_append_from_mbs_in_codepage() no longer passes MB_PRECOMPOSED
   with it.

Each change is marked LIBARCHIVE_NET_NARROW_UTF8, the marker that LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES
gives this patch: patch_libarchive checks that every file the patch changes has it.

